{"qid":"Q3045696","names":["Regency of Algiers"],"title":"Regency of Algiers","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Regency of Algiers was an Ottoman tributary state on the North African coast established by the Barbarossa brothers — Aruj and Hayreddin — who seized Algiers from its local rulers in 1516; after Aruj was killed fighting Spain in 1518, Hayreddin accepted Ottoman suzerainty and Algiers became an eyalet of the empire in 1521. Governance evolved into a unique military oligarchy: by 1659 a Janissary coup ended Ottoman-appointed pashas, and thereafter the state was ruled by deys elected from a Janissary council (diwan), operating as a de facto independent maritime republic sustained by corsair activity in the Mediterranean. The Regency's corsairs raided European shipping and coastlines across the sixteenth through early nineteenth centuries, prompting punitive expeditions from the Dutch (1624), English (1655), and Americans (Barbary Wars, 1801–1815) with limited effect. France invaded in June 1830, ostensibly over a diplomatic affront, capturing Algiers on 5 July 1830 and ending the Regency after more than three centuries.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Regency's core urban and coastal territory is well-documented through Ottoman, European diplomatic, and corsair records; inland extent into the Saharan and Algerian hinterland is approximate and changed with tribal allegiances.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Regency of Algiers","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regency_of_Algiers"},{"label":"Britannica: Algiers — History","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Algiers/History"},